      content:
          '**Pathophysiology of Autonomic Dysreflexia (AD)**:\n- Occurs in SCI at **T6 and above** (this patient is T4 — at risk).\n- A **noxious stimulus below the level of injury** triggers a massive, unmodulated sympathetic response through intact thoracolumbar sympathetic outflow (T5-L2).\n- The sympathetic surge causes widespread **vasoconstriction below the injury level**, resulting in severe hypertension.\n- The brain detects the hypertension via intact baroreceptors (carotid sinus, aortic arch) and attempts compensation via **parasympathetic activation** (vagus nerve) — causing bradycardia, vasodilation, and sweating **above** the injury level.\n- However, the descending inhibitory signals from the brainstem **cannot pass the spinal cord lesion** to reach the thoracolumbar sympathetics, so the vasoconstriction below the lesion persists.\n- Result: **Severe hypertension with reflex bradycardia**. The BP can exceed 250/130 mmHg — risking **hemorrhagic stroke, seizure, retinal hemorrhage, myocardial ischemia, and death**.\n\n**Immediate Management Algorithm**:\n\n**Step 1: Sit the patient upright** (if not already)\n- Lowers BP by approximately 15-20 mmHg through orthostatic effect\n- Lower legs off bed if possible\n- Loosen any tight clothing, abdominal binders, compression stockings\n\n**Step 2: Monitor BP every 2-3 minutes continuously**\n\n**Step 3: Systematic trigger identification and elimination (in order of frequency)**:\n\n**Urological (80-85% of causes)**:\n1. Check for bladder distension — this patient has a palpable bladder to umbilicus\n2. If indwelling catheter: check for kinks, blockage, or displacement. Irrigate gently. Replace if not draining.\n3. If no catheter (this patient): **Catheterize immediately**. Use **2% lidocaine jelly** instilled into urethra 2-3 minutes before catheterization (urethral instrumentation without anesthetic can worsen AD). Drain slowly — rapid decompression can cause hypotension.\n4. If catheterization fails: suprapubic aspiration or consider UTI as trigger.\n\n**Gastrointestinal (next most common)**:\n5. If bladder drainage does not resolve AD: check for **fecal impaction**. Apply **2% lidocaine gel to the rectum and perianal area**, wait 2-3 minutes, then perform a gentle digital rectal exam. If impaction is present, gently disimpact.\n6. Assess for acute abdomen — appendicitis, cholecystitis, bowel obstruction.\n\n**Integumentary**:\n7. Check skin thoroughly — ingrown toenails, pressure injuries, burns, tight clothing, restrictive dressings, sharp objects in shoe or wheelchair cushion.\n\n**Other triggers**: Urinary tract infection, epididymitis, kidney stones, fractures below the injury level, heterotopic ossification, DVT, labor (in females), surgical procedures.\n\n**Step 4: Pharmacologic management** (if BP remains >150 systolic after trigger elimination, or while searching for trigger):\n\n1. **Nitropaste (2% nitroglycerin ointment)**: Apply 1-inch strip above the injury level. Rapid onset (minutes). Easy to remove if hypotension occurs. First-line pharmacologic agent.\n2. **Nifedipine immediate-release 10 mg**: Bite and swallow (NOT sublingual — absorption is unpredictable). Onset 10-15 minutes. Caution: can cause precipitous hypotension in patients with baseline low BP.\n3. **Captopril 25 mg sublingual**: Alternative to nifedipine. Onset 15-30 minutes.\n4. **Hydralazine 10-20 mg IV**: For severe refractory AD. Onset 10-20 minutes.\n5. **Labetalol 20 mg IV**: Caution — can worsen bradycardia (use only if HR is not already significantly low).\n\n**For This Patient**:\n- He is already upright in wheelchair\n- Bladder distension is the most likely trigger (last catheterized 5 hours ago, palpable bladder)\n- **Immediate action**: Catheterize with lidocaine jelly, drain slowly\n- Apply nitropaste while preparing for catheterization\n- BP should drop within minutes of bladder drainage\n- If BP remains elevated after drainage, check for fecal impaction (he reported incomplete bowel program)\n- Monitor for 2 hours after resolution (triggers can recur)',
