ning for every staff member on this unit. And fourth, I am going to make sure your nurse today — and I am not criticizing her personally, because she was not given the training she needed — receives one-on-one education about AD before she cares for another SCI patient."\n\n**6. Support his right to file a complaint.**\n"You said you want to file a complaint, and you absolutely should. Patient safety reports are how hospitals identify and fix problems. I will help you do that if you want, or I can connect you with patient relations. I will also be filing an internal safety report on my end. Your experience today should lead to changes that protect you and every future SCI patient on this unit."\n\n**7. Rebuild trust.**\n"I know today shook your trust in us, and I understand that. I cannot undo what happened, but I can promise you that I heard you, I believe you, and I am going to act on it. You deserve to feel safe in this hospital, and I am going to work to earn that trust back."\n\n**Key Principles**: Validated justified anger without defensiveness. Acknowledged the genuine safety threat. Took institutional responsibility. Offered specific, actionable solutions (not platitudes). Supported the patient\'s right to formal complaint. Separated individual nurse education gaps from personal blame (systems failure, not character failure). Committed to follow-through.',
